**Design summary**

| Concern | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Encryption at rest | AES-256-GCM (confidentiality + integrity in one primitive) |
| Key derivation | PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256, 100 000 iterations, random 16-byte salt stored in the KV backend |
| Owner / name isolation | Owner + name are fed as GCM **Additional Authenticated Data** (length-prefixed, unambiguous); a ciphertext encrypted for one (owner, name) pair cannot be decrypted for any other |
| Tamper detection | GCM's 128-bit authentication tag is verified on every `get`; any bit-flip, truncation, or cross-secret copy causes `crypto.subtle.decrypt` to throw, which is re-thrown as a generic `Error` |
| Wrong master password | The derived AES key differs, so every GCM tag check fails and `get` throws |
| Storage key collisions | Owner and name are base64-encoded (output alphabet `[A-Za-z0-9+/=]`) and joined with `\x01`, which never appears in base64; the salt key uses a distinct `\x00`-delimited prefix so it is never matched by `list` |
| `list` | Scans `storage.keys()`, filters by the owner prefix, decodes names, and sorts with the default `Array.prototype.sort` |
| `remove` | Checks key existence in storage; deletes on hit, returns `false` on miss |
| Empty values | `enc.encode("")` produces a 0-byte input; GCM still produces a 16-byte tag, so the round-trip works |
| Persistence | All state (salt + ciphertexts) lives in the caller-supplied `KeyValueStorage`; a fresh `createSecretStore` with the same password and storage re-derives the same key and reads everything |

